Well known. The issue has never been what the
compiler will do, but what the next coder will
understand.

> And casting of NULL is a horrible idea--it can
> never cause correct behavior, but CAN suppress
> warnings if someone has defined NULL to something
> stupid--which I've seen happen before with people
> who didn't understand that pointers and longs can
> be different sizes on some machines.

I disagree.

Folks who would #define NULL, like my 1st wife who
dumped unleaded into a diesel auto's gas tank (and
burned out the engine) are at a level of technical
illiteracy that no amount of effort can protect from
themselves.

The point of the cast, again, isn't to _do_
anything at run time, it is to alert the _next code
maintainer_, maybe a retread APL programmer, that
what is being compared against isn't "any old NULL"
but a NULL of the type compliant variety.

Why does that possibly matter? Because if the base
type of the variable gets redefined, it will, in a
good compiler, throw an error message about type
incompatibility, alerting that "next maintainer"
that here is a portion of the code that might
very well require rethinking.

I cannot begin to imagine how much of the code I
have written as strong typing became available is in
the source for the sole purpose of forcing the
compiler to scream at me if some assumption I've
made is incorrect or becomes incorrect, and not at
all there to produce object code.  This is a very
strong part of defensive coding.

Making it possible is a vary strong part of why
strong typing got introduced and implemented by the
computer language design gurus.

Since C "strong typing" is incredibly weak,
installing code decorations to take advantage of
what type safety C provides is frequently worth the
effort.

